{"packet_id":"7019e36f01a45710","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A1 separates the objective (whether evidence justifies investment) from the suggested consumer move. It lists real alternatives, including B2B expansion, no expansion and licensing for royalties, and states budget and time constraints. It does not identify a missed constraint or correct a premise, for example the gap between a clinical-validation population and home users."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A11","A17"],"reason":"The best theory, T1 (A9), says who changes behaviour (health-conscious home users) and why (they value reliable body-fat data). The value-capture step rests on 'medical-grade accuracy' and premium pricing or subscriptions that no source supports. The accuracy link is weakened by the wide 2SD in tanita-pm-jebb (A11), so the full chain is not traced soundly."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 and T2 share the same product, customer and channel, and differ only in marketing framing. T2's mechanism is incoherent: it promises results 'without the need to stand on a scale' and 'without the hassle of standing' for a stand-on device (A10). Both theories carry an identical 30% purchase-intent prediction. In effect these are rewordings plus a nonsense variant."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"The assumptions are vague, for example 'willing to pay for health-related products' and 'marketing can communicate'. The first prediction is identical across T1 and T2, so it cannot put either theory in trouble. The willingness-to-pay predictions ('at least 20%' and '15% of the price of a comparable commercial scale') are claimed to indicate a premium, which is self-contradictory."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A2","A11","A12","A15","A17"],"reason":"A11 correctly notes that tanita-pm-jebb does not show demand. However, it treats the study as support for trustworthy accuracy despite a 2SD of 7.9 kg for individuals, and gives no date, place or population fit. tanita-pm-radley (not recommended for individual overweight/obese children) and tanita-pm-lukaski1985 (further validation needed for abnormal body composition) were retrieved in A2 but never assessed. The 'brand reputation' and 'household name' company claim (tanita-en-share) is used as positioning support in A15 and A17 without being labelled a company assertion."},"seeking_challenge":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A13","A2"],"reason":"A13 runs a search against T1, but the findings are never reported or weighed. The clearest contrary evidence on individual accuracy, tanita-pm-radley from A2, is ignored entirely."},"experiment_design":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A14"],"reason":"A14 compares only relative preference between two framings, one of which is incoherent. Its decision rule maps 'no difference between framings' to 'no expansion', so it cannot separate 'demand for both' from 'demand for neither' on the actual investment question. Stated intent stands in for behaviour. Other flaws: an online panel in 1993 is anachronistic; the sample is limited to English-speaking regions despite the Japanese home market; and 'BMI range (16–78 kg/m2)' confuses jebb's age range with BMI. The €18,000/10-week cost is an assumption, not an observation."},"revision":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A16"],"reason":"The delivered update SYN-D is simulated warehouse data and irrelevant to wearers. A16 correctly retains T1 with an evidence-linked reason. It does not explicitly trace implications for the recommendation, though none were needed."},"recommendation":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A15","A17","A14"],"reason":"A17 commits to developing the proposition while admitting demand is uncertain and the test is still to come. This does not follow from the evidence. It asserts 'medical-grade accuracy' that the sources do not support, and its target age range of 18–88 matches no source. It does state a next action and reversal conditions, but the reversal conditions inherit the flawed decision rule from A14."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A10"],"reason":"T2's mechanism claims results without standing for a stand-on device; this is nonsense offered as a mechanism."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A9","A11","A17"],"reason":"'Medical-grade accuracy' and trustworthiness for home users are inferred from tanita-pm-jebb despite a 2SD of 7.9 kg. Unassessed contrary sources (tanita-pm-radley, tanita-pm-lukaski1985) undercut it. This hypothesis is stated as fact in the recommendation."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A15","A17"],"reason":"The company claim of market share and household-name status (tanita-en-share) is used as positioning support for a consumer proposition without being labelled as an assertion, and it says nothing about body-fat demand."},{"kind":"non_discriminating_test","event_ids":["A9","A10","A14"],"reason":"The rivals share an identical intent prediction. The test measures only relative framing preference, and its 'neither condition met → no expansion' rule cannot distinguish equal demand from no demand on the investment decision."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A14"],"reason":"The test design is infeasible or confused: an online panel in 1993, 'BMI 16–78 kg/m2' copied from jebb's age range, and an English-speaking sample mismatched to the Japanese home market."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A17"],"reason":"The target age range of 18–88 is unsupported by any source."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"The episode is complete but contains critical errors: an incoherent rival mechanism (A10), unsupported 'medical-grade accuracy' claims with contrary evidence ignored (A11, A17), a test whose decision rule cannot separate the investment alternatives (A14), and a recommendation to invest ahead of evidence. Revision of irrelevant simulated data was handled correctly (A16), but that does not offset these errors."}
